{
  /**
   * The browser suite's own program.
   *
   * **`e2e/` had no compile-time gate at all.** `tsconfig.json` is the
   * application (browser types only) and `tsconfig.test.json` includes
   * `src/**‍/*.test.ts` and nothing else, so twenty-odd Playwright files —
   * fixtures, the security matrix, the budgets — were typechecked by nobody.
   * Playwright transpiles without checking, so a mistake there surfaces as a
   * runtime failure partway through a fifteen-minute run, in whichever spec
   * happened to touch it.
   *
   * It is not hypothetical: the first run of this program found a
   * `spendLogin` used in `security.spec.ts` and imported nowhere, which would
   * have failed three browser tests with `spendLogin is not defined` after
   * ten minutes of green.
   *
   * A third program rather than widening the second, for the reason the
   * second exists: these files need `node` *and* `@playwright/test` globals,
   * and letting the application share those would make `fs` and `process`
   * typecheck inside a React component — where they resolve at compile time
   * and are absent at runtime, so the mistake ships as a blank page.
   *
   * `auth.spec.ts` imports `REFRESH_GRACE_MS` from `server/src`, which is
   * deliberate — a duplicated thirty seconds would go stale the first time
   * somebody tuned it — so the server tree is reachable from here and is
   * checked as part of this program.
   */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "types": ["node"],
    "noEmit": true,
    /*
      The server is compiled by its own `tsc` with its own settings; this
      program only needs to *read* the one constant `auth.spec.ts` imports.
      Without this, its decorators and Nest types would be checked here under
      the wrong options and report errors the server's own build does not.
    */
    "skipLibCheck": true
  },
  "include": ["e2e/**/*.ts", "playwright.config.ts"],
  "exclude": []
}
